meter of the of the  fiber  up to 12 microliter so that's 20 times  more  phase volume so we have a much larger  absorption phase volume and that allows  you to  collect more material which then  enhances your sensitivity  and that's exactly what we see so here  we're looking at a chromatogram now of  typical  aroma compounds in coffee  bottom chromatogram being the  conventional spami  the top chromatogram being the speedy  arrow and you can see very easily just  looking at the size of the peaks that  this beamy arrow gives us a much better  sensitivity  and it's even easier to see in this  graph here where in  the blue bars or this beamy arrow the  orange bars are the conventionals be me  and the  bars have been normalized to this beamy  arrow so those are always one and so  these are typical aroma compounds that  you will find in coffee  and as you can see this beamy arrow  gives anywhere from three to about ten  fold  increase in sensitivity of the speedy  era over the conventional steamy  okay so now that we've had our coffee we  can start thinking about lunch and we  start cooking our lunch  as we all know if you ever walked into a  kitchen there the the food changes uh  smell as you start cooking it  and that is the result of the the mayo  reaction and that's basically a reaction  between the  amino acids in the proteins in the food  and the reducing sugars and that gives  sort of these characteristic  smell of cooked food  in this particular experiment we are  comparing  unheated and cooked soy sauce  and you see on the right hand side this  um contraption that we built and it's a  enclosed uh  vessel and inside is a mono trap solvent  which is a monolithic  silica adsorbent that will then absorb  those  volatile aroma compounds and if you look  closely you'll see that there is a  shield or a guard around that uh solvent  disc to protect it from uh soy sauce  splashing up because there's you know  little uh milliliter uh milliliter or so  of soy sauce on the bottom of this  heating uh device and we don't want any  splashes to go straight onto the solvent  disc but we only want to trap the aroma  compounds that are released  but once the  that's heated up the aroma compounds are  released then we'll do a simple solvent  extraction of the the mono trap  and when we do that we see immediately  the big differences between the the  unheated and then the cooked soy sauce  so  the pink is the unheated and the black  is the heated and you immediately see  the differences some compounds will be  reduced like peaks number eight and 10  for instance and then other com  compounds are increased like peaks  31  and 35 and we'll see on the next slides  what these are  so  there are uh  compounds that are distinctive for the  cooked soy sauce and there are  different categories there's alcohols as  you can see there's aldehydes there's  the pyroxenes and then and then some  others  and then there's also aroma compounds  that are very distinctive for the  unheated soy sauce those are on the top  right  so those are the compounds that we would  associate with just smelling soy sauce  in a bottle versus smelling soy sauce on  fried rice or fried noodles or things  like this and then on the bottom right  are compounds that are common to both  heated and unheated soy sauce and  because  even heated or unheated we can still  recognize uh soy sauce the smell the  aroma of soy sauce so there are  compounds that are common to both  and we have those summarized here so  the main compounds of the unheated soils  are so all these  butanol and butanol type compounds so  butanol isobutanol  three methyl one butanol so that's what  we  that's what we smell when we smell just  soy sauce as you have it here say as a  dipping sauce for dumplings or or  sushi or so  then once we cook it then we get these  uh reaction products of the mayor  reaction the fulfill alcohol fulfilled  pyrrole pyrozines those kind of  compounds and those we know we associate  them with uh cooked soy sauce like we  have here like fried rice or kampao  chicken or any of those foods that have  cooked soy sauce in them  and so  the results didn't really say that hey  the character of the soy sauce changes  as we start heating it so the character  of soy sauce decreases and we're  creating these fragrant smells  as a result of the of the cooking  so we know that  okay now that we've had coffee and lunch  it's time to think about happy hour and  here we're looking at uh  lemon flavored alcoholic beverages like  uh you know like a lemon like a lemon  vodka or something like that  so those  samples were stored in the refrigerator  prior to opening once we opened them we  put them in a vial with our trusty mono  trap solvent that we've used for these  previous experiments  and then we start heating it up and we  we trap the fragrance components uh in  the uh  monosome and trap and then we'll do a  solvent extraction and again analyze by  gcms  and here's what we'll see so there's  these are the four different uh  lemon flavored alcoholic beverages and  you can see that there's a lot of common  peaks especially at the beginning of the  chromatogram but there are also uh  differences and some of the differences  are you know the the monoterpenes that  we see there like limonene mercene  there are sesame terpenes that we find  like the caryophyllene  uh terpenoids monoterpene alcohols uh  like linolol  and then charanil acetate so a lot of  different uh these kind of flavor  compounds have been identified in some  but not all and especially the aliphatic  aldehydes and we'll see on the next  slide  specifically what these are and i don't  need to go through all of them but you  can see that some of them are very  characteristic of some of the beverages  while others are not in on the  on the column on the right you can see  uh the different  classes of compounds that they're in  but then we uh we went a step further  and we did our principle principle  component analysis pca  and we really see the distinct  differences between all four of those  beverages and you see the colored circle  correspond to the colors of the  chromatogram a couple of slides ago  and you can see that each of the  beverages the  the compounds fall within distinct  clusters and and that can be used in in  the product development i mean  ultimately ultimately the the sensory  testing like this this woman here you  know sampling  the beverage  that's what's important how we perceive  it our sensory perception but  as  the companies develop these products you  know gcms can be used to  cooperate those sensory testing results  and really help i help identify and  categorize and provide a  objective measurement throughout the  product development process to make sure  that there is a consistent product  quality in in the end  okay so  um  sulfur compounds  so as i mentioned on the first slide  our  nose is a very sensitive detector and  especially when it comes to sulfur  compounds and  we often associate sulfur compounds with  unpleasant smell you know like the  typical rotten egg smell that that's uh  so famous for sulphur compounds  but we also saw in this in the um coffee  and we'll see here in the following  slides that there are also sulfur  compounds that contribute positively to  the aroma and the smell of a food  but again our nose is very sensitive to  these sulfur compounds and sometimes the  the gcms is not sensitive enough to pick  those up so we'll have to go to a more  dedicated detector and in this case  we'll be using a sulfur  chemiluminescence detector and scd that  really offers excellent  sensitivity for sulfur compounds even at  low trace levels  and on the bottom right you'll see our  little setup so we're using kimchi as  our sample that's the  famous korean pickled cabbage and it's  in a sealed bag in this little container  and we have our mono trap there again so  as we're heating this up we're trapping  these uh  kimchi flavors and then we'll do  a solvent extraction again  and so what we'll see then is we'll have  this dual uh detector approach on the  bottom you have the gcms a total ion  chromatogram  on the top you have the gcse the sulfur  chemical luminescent detector and you  can see much bigger peak so it's much  easier to quantify these sulfur  compounds with an sed than with the mass  spec in particular if you look at peaks  four or five now you have to look very  closely to find them in the mass back  trace versus the sed where they're very  prominent so  with the sed a dedicated sulfur detector  you'll pick those up  keep in mind though that that detector  is specific for the sulfur compound so  if you look at the chromatogram there  are a couple of large peaks that are  between four and six minutes  those peaks must not contain sulfur ions  because you don't  see them in the in the scd chromatogram  so  anything that's not sulfur related of  course is lost within with an sct  chromatogram  so i remember we took it to the next  step and this is something that i'm sure  you've all experienced if you have  a food in a in a tupperware type of  container you can not only stain but  some of the the food smell will remain  behind so we actually did the experiment  here so we we took the the kimchi  container we washed it with water and  then we cut it into little pieces put  the little pieces in the little uh jar  and uh heated it up and we captured  whatever  was released all the molecules with a  speedy fiber in this case when we  weren't using the monitor we're actually  using a multi-phase speed me that was a  dvd divinely benzene carbon and td ms  polydimethyl cyloxane so this multi-mode  speed me will capture the sulfur that's  released from the  pieces of the container to see what is  what is left behind even after rinsing  with water  so again we're comparing the the scd  with the gcms and in this case we broke  the chromatogram which is about 30 some  minutes long into three  sections and and you can see here on the  the first segments again you see  immediately like peaks four uh much  greater sensitivity on the  on the sed compared to the uh to the  gcms  and then here again this is the middle  section and you can see some of the  peaks that are getting much more  pronounced like peak  19 or 22 in particular is much greater  on the uh  on the sad than it is on on the gcms  and here is the the tail end of the  chromatogram and you can see a lot of  these compounds 29  32  those are all much much  more pronounced on the  sulfur chemical menescence detector  compared to the  to the mass  spec so again it just shows that  the mass that the um  we'll go to the here's just the uh the  this all the different compounds if you  wanted to look at what all of these 36  compounds were  but here again a total of 36 we saw and  in but were identified in the wash  kimchi container and you can see here on  the top on the left is the  scd trace on the right is the gcms and  you can see that  because the scd only looks at sulfur  compounds there's no interference like  you would have on the mass spec so you  have some interference there that may or  may not give you as accurate a  quantitation of those sulfur compounds  and the same you see on the bottom you  get a  a simpler chromatogram if you will with  the scd because it is very selective and  because of that it's also very sensitive  and so it's an excellent choice for  trace sulfur compound analysis  and now for something completely  different  brought to you by the ministry of silly  walks  so  some foods  you don't  smell until you actually  bite into them for instance on the  bottom right you see this this woman  smelling the apple in the store but you  don't really smell much off an apple  until you actually take a bite  out of that apple and then you start  getting some of the uh aroma compounds  released and  so  we can actually  measure that and you see the instrument  that's used  on the on the right there it's a it's  our uh what we call the chore and and  here is a a  kind of a cartoon of how it works but  first it before we get back to the apple  if you look at the right hand side this  this chewer is actually used by a lot of  food development companies in this case  it's like the  beyond meat  where they they look at their uh  plant-based burger and compare the  texture to a meat-based  hamburger and so you simulate the the  chewing with this machine and it gives  you a  sense of the  texture so it's it's really pretty cool  i like those instruments because you can  actually  see what's going on unlike a  chromatograph you know you make an  injection and you know you don't see  anything  so  or  to the left of that you see the the sure  there so it's a a way of simulating the  uh the chewing but we can again take  that another step further by  putting that inside a  sample bag and so not only now are we  simulating the chewing and getting a  feel for the texture  but now we can capture  these flavor compounds as they are being  released while we are chewing so it's a  i guess they like the experience that  we're actually getting as we're eating  the food  and again we're trapping it on a speed  me unit and then analyzing it on the uh  on the gcms  so just to summarize the method here  again flavors are released during the  chewing process  and we can detect them subjectively by  the nodes but we can also uh measure  them objectively with the true brands so  compounds are collected by speedy and  then analyzed by  by gcms  but before we get to the to the flavor  so this is kind of interesting that you  can do  simultaneously look at the texture and  the flavor so on the top right where you  see the word brittle this actually  measures the texture so  there's  see you see there's five peaks so those  are five different chews off the apple  and that'll give you an idea of the the  crispiness or so if you will of the  apple and we'll see  the results on the on the next page  at the same time then we trap what is  being released in each of those choose  and then we'll do the the flavor  analysis by gcms and you see on the top  left  the red trace which is now the  after the the tube compared to what the  at the bottom is before the chewing  and you can see  uh big changes  in the size of the peak so these are the  compounds that are being released  during the the chewing  so  in this case again we're looking at  comparison of two species of apples and  so the the top one  because you can see the the peaks or the  i guess they are peaks they're larger so  that indicates that that apple is firmer  it's more crisp compared to the one on  the bottom that is more soft and chewy  so these are two different apples and we  can determine the the chewiness and how  firm the apple is  and then we trap the compounds  and so here we have the two different  apples so you have apple uh species not  a on the top  apple species  b  on the bottom and you can see that there  are some compounds  most of them are alcohol esters not  surprisingly  and some compounds if you look are  identical so that would be  the compounds that we would associate  with an apple flavor as opposed to a  pear flavor or a peach or any other  fruit so that's what we recognize as  apple  but there are also differences between  apple species a and b and that those are  the compounds that we would use to  differentiate between you know say a  golden delicious or a gala or fuji or  granny smith or any of these  apple species that we typically eat so  we can at a molecular level for after  the chewing collect these these  aroma compounds and then identify the  apple species just based  based on that  and that's that's all i had for today uh  i hope you you found it interesting  certainly the the chemistry  smell is very important you know we we  eat with our eyes as you know if you  look at the picture on the left there  with all the deliciou