s used  on the on the right there it's a it's  our uh what we call the chore and and  here is a a  kind of a cartoon of how it works but  first it before we get back to the apple  if you look at the right hand side this  this chewer is actually used by a lot of  food development companies in this case  it's like the  beyond meat  where they they look at their uh  plant-based burger and compare the  texture to a meat-based  hamburger and so you simulate the the  chewing with this machine and it gives  you a  sense of the  texture so it's it's really pretty cool  i like those instruments because you can  actually  see what's going on unlike a  chromatograph you know you make an  injection and you know you don't see  anything  so  or  to the left of that you see the the sure  there so it's a a way of simulating the  uh the chewing but we can again take  that another step further by  putting that inside a  sample bag and so not only now are we  simulating the chewing and getting a  feel for the texture  but now we can capture  these flavor compounds as they are being  released while we are chewing so it's a  i guess they like the experience that  we're actually getting as we're eating  the food  and again we're trapping it on a speed  me unit and then analyzing it on the uh  on the gcms  so just to summarize the method here  again flavors are released during the  chewing process  and we can detect them subjectively by  the nodes but we can also uh measure  them objectively with the true brands so  compounds are collected by speedy and  then analyzed by  by gcms  but before we get to the to the flavor  so this is kind of interesting that you  can do  simultaneously look at the texture and  the flavor so on the top right where you  see the word brittle this actually  measures the texture so  there's  see you see there's five peaks so those  are five different chews off the apple  and that'll give you an idea of the the  crispiness or so if you will of the  apple and we'll see  the results on the on the next page  at the same time then we trap what is  being released in each of those choose  and then we'll do the the flavor  analysis by gcms and you see on the top  left  the red trace which is now the  after the the tube compared to what the  at the bottom is before the chewing  and you can see  uh big changes  in the size of the peak so these are the  compounds that are being released  during the the chewing  so  in this case again we're looking at  comparison of two species of apples and  so the the top one  because you can see the the peaks or the  i guess they are peaks they're larger so  that indicates that that apple is firmer  it's more crisp compared to the one on  the bottom that is more soft and chewy  so these are two different apples and we  can determine the the chewiness and how  firm the apple is  and then we trap the compounds  and so here we have the two different  apples so you have apple uh species not  a on the top  apple species  b  on the bottom and you can see that there  are some compounds  most of them are alcohol esters not  surprisingly  and some compounds if you look are  identical so that would be  the compounds that we would associate  with an apple flavor as opposed to a  pear flavor or a peach or any other  fruit so that's what we recognize as  apple  but there are also differences between  apple species a and b and that those are  the compounds that we would use to  differentiate between you know say a  golden delicious or a gala or fuji or  granny smith or any of these  apple species that we typically eat so  we can at a molecular level for after  the chewing collect these these  aroma compounds and then identify the  apple species just based  based on that  and that's that's all i had for today uh  i hope you you found it interesting  certainly the the chemistry  smell is very important you know we we  eat with our eyes as you know if you  look at the picture on the left there  with all the deliciou